  "prompt": "prompts/physics_committee_luna_round2_v1.md",
  "preferred_configuration": {
    "prize_parts": [
      {
        "ballot_id": "B005",
        "laureates": [
          "Hidetoshi Katori",
          "Jun Ye"
        ],
        "citation": "For the development of optical lattice clocks, enabling unprecedented precision in time measurement and new tests of fundamental physics."
      }
    ]
  },
  "alternatives": [
    "B003",
    "B008"
  ],
  "statement": {
    "case_for": "I retain optical lattice clocks as my preferred achievement. They turn a well-defined atomic transition into a stable ruler for time. Independent experimental realizations and sustained precision gains establish maturity, while the clocks enable tests of fundamental physics as well as improved time standards. Katori and Ye have complementary, identifiable contributions that support focused credit.",
    "case_against": "The committee must distinguish the foundational clock advances from the broader development of optical frequency standards and later improvements. There is also a question of whether the central achievement should be framed as a fundamental physics discovery or as a precision measurement technique. The case should rest on demonstrated scientific capability, not predictions about future applications.",
    "responses": [
      {
        "member_id": "johansson-goran",
        "point": "Johansson argues that quantum error correction is a mature conceptual contribution because it established that quantum information can be protected in principle, even though scalable fault-tolerant operation remains unfinished.",
        "response": "I agree that the conceptual result need not wait for a full-scale machine. That distinction makes quantum error correction a serious candidate. I still favor the clocks because their foundational advance is paired with a long record of repeated experimental performance. The breadth of the early error-correction contributions also leaves a more difficult question about the scope and attribution of one prize."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "mehlig-bernhard",
        "point": "Mehlig favors fluctuation relations as a coherent conceptual achievement, arguing that their general physical principle is a stronger basis for recognition than a measurement technology whose future applications are developing.",
        "response": "The general constraints on nonequilibrium behavior are a substantial contribution, and I agree that they have value beyond particular applications. Their layered history and the relationship among the Evans, Gallavotti-Cohen, and Jarzynski results make the prize scope and individual attribution uncertain. For the clocks, the physical advance, experimental validation, and proposed credit form a more bounded case."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "olsson-eva",
        "point": "Olsson argues that the optical lattice clock case can rest on established precision measurement and tests of physics without relying on predictions about future applications, while noting that the original advances must be separated from later improvements.",
        "response": "I agree. The demonstrated precision and scientific tests are sufficient grounds for the case; speculative applications should not carry it. The historical boundary remains important, and the citation should recognize the clock advances and their experimental realization without implying credit for all subsequent improvements."
      }
    ],
    "uncertainties": [
      "The historical record should clarify how Katori's and Ye's original clock contributions divide and how they relate to later developments in optical frequency standards.",
      "The committee should decide how to describe the achievement as a fundamental advance in precision measurement and tests of physics, rather than relying on the eventual reach of its applications."
    ],
    "conflict_note": "No personal, institutional, or coauthor connection to Katori or Ye is apparent from the supplied profile. My expertise in theoretical atomic physics is relevant to assessing the scientific case, but I have no disclosed relationship to the proposed laureates."
  }
}
